== Refinements of the Brauer Correspondence
<sec:brauer-correspondence-refinements>

=== Blocks and Normal Subgroups <ss:blocks-normal-subgroups>

In this subsection, $R$ denotes either a field of characteristic $p$, or a
d.v.r. with maximal ideal $frak(p)$ such that $R$ is complete in the
$frak(p)$-adic topology and the residue field $R slash frak(p)$ has
characteristic $p$. We shall study the connections between $p$-blocks of $G$ and
$p$-blocks of a normal subgroup $H$ of $G$, and their defect groups. Some
results in this direction were obtained earlier, for the special case of a
normal $p$-subgroup (see @prop:normal-p-subgroup-block-idempotents). Our
discussion is based on the work of Brauer @bib:Brauer1959a, Alperin–Burry
@bib:Alperin1980, and Alperin–Broué @bib:Alperin1979, and uses many of the ideas
introduced in §@ss:brauer-correspondence-general for the module-theoretic
approach to the Brauer correspondence.

We first introduce some notation, which will be used throughout this subsection.
We always let $H$ denote a normal subgroup of $G$. Then $G$ acts by conjugation
on the set of block idempotents of $R H$. For each block idempotent
$b' in c(R H)$, we set
$ "Stab"_G b'={g in G:attach(b', tl: g)=b'}, $
where $attach(b, tl: g)=g b g^(-1)$ as usual. Since $b'$ is the unique central
idempotent in its block ideal $B'$, we also have
$ "Stab"_G b'={g in G:attach(B', tl: g)=B'}="Stab"_G B'. $
For each block ideal $B'$ in $R H$, we let $R(G B' G)$ denote the two-sided
ideal in $R G$ generated by $B'$. Then $R(G B' G)$ consists of all $R$-linear
combinations of the elements ${x a y:x,y in G,a in B'}$.

We recall the left action of $R(G times G)$ on $R G$, given by
$(x,y)a=x a y^(-1)$, for $(x,y) in G times G$ and $a in R G$. A left
$R(G times G)$-module $M$ defines a left $R(H times H)$-module by restriction of
scalars, as well as a left $R H$-module, by restriction of scalars from $R G$ to
$R H$. We denote these restrictions by
$ M_(H times H) quad "and" quad M_(H times 1), $
respectively.

We shall find it useful to consider certain subgroups of $G times G$ which are
related to the stabilizers of the block ideals ${B'}$ of $R H$, and are defined
by
$ T=T(B')=(H times H)Delta("Stab"_G B'), $
#source(501)
where $Delta:G -> G times G$ is the diagonal map. For each block ideal $B'$ of
$R H$, and $T=T(B')$, we have $T dot B' subset.eq B'$; we then let $tilde(B)'$
denote the block ideal $B'$ viewed as an $R T$-module.

#definition[
  Let $B$ be a block ideal of $R G$, and $B'$ a block ideal of $R H$. We shall
  say that $B$ _covers_ $B'$ whenever $B' divides B_(H times H)$. In this case,
  we also say that $upright(sans(B))$ covers $upright(sans(B))'$, where
  $upright(sans(B))$ and $upright(sans(B))'$ are the corresponding blocks of $G$
  and $H$, respectively.
] <def:covering-blocks>
#idx("Covering blocks", target: [@def:covering-blocks])
#idx("p-Blocks", "and normal subgroups", target: [@def:covering-blocks])

In the statement and proof of the theorem below, the defect groups of block
idempotents will also be called the defect groups of the corresponding block
ideals.

Note that if the Brauer correspondent $(upright(sans(B))')^G$ is defined for a
$p$-block $upright(sans(B))'$ of $H$, then the block
$upright(sans(B))=(upright(sans(B))')^G$ covers $upright(sans(B))'$, by
@prop:brauer-correspondent-block-summand. Later (see
@cor:covering-block-module-criterion) we shall obtain a criterion for one block
to cover another, in terms of modules in the blocks.

Our aim is to prove:

#theorem[
  (Alperin–Burry @bib:Alperin1980).
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[Each block ideal $B$ of $R G$ covers exactly one $G$-conjugacy class
      of block ideals of $R H$.] <case:covering-block-conjugacy-class>],
    [#case[Let $B$ cover $B'$, and let $D$ be a defect group of $B$. Then
      $D inter H =_G D'$, for some defect group $D'$ of $B'$. Moreover,
      $D <=_G "Stab"_G B'$.] <case:covering-block-defect-intersection>],
    [#case[Let $B'$ be a block ideal of $R H$ which is covered by some block
        ideal of $R G$. Then there is a block ideal $B_0$ of $R G$ such that
        $B_0$ covers $B'$, whose defect group $D_0$ is the unique maximal
        element among the defect groups of all block ideals covering $B'$, with
        respect to the order relation $<=_G$.]
      <case:covering-block-maximal-defect>],
    [#case[Let $D'$ be a defect group of $B'$, and assume that
        $H >= C_G lr((D'))$. Then $(upright(sans(B))')^G$ is defined, and is the
        unique block of $G$ covering $upright(sans(B))'$.]
      <case:covering-block-centralizer-uniqueness>],
  )
] <th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks>

We begin with some preliminary results.

#lemma[
  Let $B'$ be a block ideal in $R H$ with block idempotent $b'$. Let $T=T(B')$
  be the subgroup of $G times G$ defined by $T=(H times H)Delta("Stab"_G B')$,
  and $tilde(B)'$ the extension of $B'$ to $T$, as described above.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[We have
        $ R(G B' G)=sum_(x in G) attach((b'), tl: x) R G. $]
      <case:normal-block-generated-ideal>],
    [#case[The left $R H$-module $(R G)_(H times 1)$ can be expressed uniquely
        as a direct sum of modules belonging to the different blocks of $H$. In
        this decomposition, the submodule $R(G B' G)_(H times 1)$ coincides with
        the direct summand associated with the conjugates of the block
        $upright(sans(B))'$.]
      <case:normal-block-left-restriction>],
    [#case[There exists a direct decomposition
        #source(502)
        $
          R(G B' G)_(H times H)=plus.o.big (x,y) B'
          quad ("as" R(H times H)"-modules"),
        $
        where the sum is taken over a cross-section of $(G times G) slash T$.]
      <case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction>],
    [#case[There is an isomorphism of $R(G times G)$-modules
        $ R(G B' G) tilde.equiv "ind"_T^(G times G) tilde(B)'. $]
      <case:normal-block-induced-bimodule>],
    [#case[A block ideal $B$ of $R G$ covers $B'$ if and only if
        $B divides R(G B' G)$ (as $R(G times G)$-modules).]
      <case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand>],
  )
] <lem:normal-block-generated-ideal>

#proof[
  @case:normal-block-generated-ideal For all $x,y in G$, we have
  $ x B' y=(x B' x^(-1))x y subset.eq attach((b'), tl: x) R G. $
  The formula @case:normal-block-generated-ideal now follows, since $R(G B' G)$
  is generated as $R$-module by the elements $x a y$, for $x,y in G$ and
  $a in B'$. The statement @case:normal-block-left-restriction is an immediate
  consequence of @case:normal-block-generated-ideal.

  @case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction It is easily checked that $(u,v)B'=B'$
  for all $(u,v) in T(B')$. It follows that the sum $sum(x, y)B'$, taken over a
  cross-section of $(G times G) slash T(B')$, is a submodule of
  $(R G)_(H times H)$. Further discussion is needed in order to prove that the
  sum is direct. We have
  $ T(B') <= (H times H)Delta G <= G times G. $
  A cross-section for the first inclusion can be chosen from $Delta G$, and for
  the second from $G times 1$. Products of elements of these cross-sections form
  a cross-section of $(G times G) slash T(B')$. Notice that
  $(x,x)B'=x B' x^(-1) subset.eq R H$ for all $x in G$; while
  $(y,1)B' subset.eq R(G-H)$ if $y in.not H$, that is $(y,1)a$ is supported
  outside $H$ for all $a in B'$. For an arbitrary element $(x,y x)$ of the
  resulting cross-section, we have $(x,y x)B' subset.eq R H$ only if $y=1$, and
  the block ideals $(x,x)B'=x B' x^(-1)$ are all different as ${(x,x)}$ ranges
  over a cross-section of $(H times H)Delta G slash T(B')$. Using these remarks,
  it is easily verified that
  $ (x,z)B' inter sum_((x',z')!=(x,z)) (x',z')B'=0 $
  for arbitrary elements ${(x,z),(x',z'),dots}$ of the cross-section, and
  @case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction is proved. Part
  @case:normal-block-induced-bimodule follows at once from part
  @case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction and the Imprimitivity Theorem
  @i:prop:induced-module-criterion.

  @case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand First suppose
  $B divides R(G B' G)$. Using part @case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction and
  the K-S-A Theorem, it follows that $(x,y)B' divides B_(H times H)$ for some
  $(x,y) in G times G$. Then $B' divides (x,y)^(-1) B_(H times H)$, and hence
  $B' divides B_(H times H)$, using the fact that $(x,y)^(-1)B=B$ for all
  $(x,y) in G times G$. This proves the implication one way.

  Conversely, suppose $B' divides B$, and let $b$ be the block idempotent in
  $B$. Then
  $ b in sum R(G B'' G), $
  #source(503)
  where the summands are two-sided ideals in $R G$ defined by the block ideals
  ${B''}$ of $R H$. By Rosenberg’s Lemma
  @lem:rosenberg-primitive-idempotent-ideal-sum, we obtain $b in R(G B'' G)$ for
  some block ideal $B''$. It is then easily checked that $B divides R(G B'' G)$.
  Since $B' divides B$ and $B divides R(G B'' G)$, we obtain
  $B' divides R(G B'' G)_(H times 1)$. By parts
  @case:normal-block-generated-ideal and @case:normal-block-left-restriction of
  the Lemma and the K-S-A Theorem, it follows that $B'$ and $B''$ are conjugate
  block ideals. Then $R(G B' G)=R(G B'' G)$ by a second application of part
  @case:normal-block-generated-ideal, and we obtain $B divides R(G B' G)$,
  completing the proof.
]

Before continuing with the proof of the main result, we obtain the following
corollary of @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal, which provides a useful
criterion for one block to cover another in terms of modules in the blocks.

#corollary[
  A block $upright(sans(B))$ of $G$ covers a block $upright(sans(B))'$ of $H$ if
  and only if there exists a left $R G$-module $M$ in $upright(sans(B))$ whose
  restriction $M_H$ has a nonzero direct summand belonging to the block
  $upright(sans(B))'$.
] <cor:covering-block-module-criterion>

#proof[
  Let $upright(sans(B))$ and $upright(sans(B))'$ be defined by block idempotents
  $b in R G$ and $b' in R H$ respectively. If $upright(sans(B))$ covers
  $upright(sans(B))'$, then clearly $b'(b R G)!=0$, and it follows that
  $(b R G)_H$ has an indecomposable direct summand $N$ such that $b' N!=0$.
  Conversely, let $M$ be a left $R G$-module in $upright(sans(B))$ such that
  $M_H$ has a nonzero direct summand belonging to $upright(sans(B))'$. Letting
  $B=b R G$, we have $b' B!=0$. By the second part of the proof of
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand,
  $B divides R(G B'' G)$ for some block ideal $B''$ of $R H$, and hence
  $b' R(G B'' G)!=0$. By part
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:normal-block-generated-ideal, it
  follows that the blocks $B'$ and $B''$ are conjugate. Then
  $R(G B'' G)=R(G B' G)$, and $B divides R(G B' G)$. By
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand,
  it follows that $upright(sans(B))$ covers $upright(sans(B))'$, completing the
  proof of the corollary.
]

#proof(head: emph[Proof of Theorem @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks.])[
  @case:covering-block-conjugacy-class By the proof of
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand,
  $B divides R(G B' G)$ for some block ideal $B'$ of $R H$, and hence $B$ covers
  at least one block ideal of $R H$. If $B$ covers another block ideal $B''$ of
  $R H$, then $B divides R(G B'' G)$ by
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand
  again, and it follows that $B'$ and $B''$ are conjugate, by
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:normal-block-generated-ideal and
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:normal-block-left-restriction. This
  completes the proof of part @case:covering-block-conjugacy-class.

  @case:covering-block-defect-intersection The proof of this part of the theorem
  is based on the interpretation of defect groups in terms of vertices and
  sources, and, in particular, the fact that block ideals are trivial source
  modules (see Exercise @exc:block-ideals-trivial-source). We begin with the
  assumption that $B$ covers $B'$, so $B' divides B_(H times H)$. If $D$ and
  $D'$ are defect groups of $B$ and $B'$, then by
  @prop:block-ideal-diagonal-vertex, $Delta D$ and $Delta D'$ are vertices of
  $B$ and $B'$, as indecomposable modules over $R(G times G)$ and
  $R(H times H)$, respectively. Since $B' divides B_(H times H)$, we have
  $Delta D' <=_(G times G) Delta D$ by @i:th:vertices-induction-restriction(i),
  and hence $D' <=_G D inter H$, using the fact that $H ⊴ G$.

  For the reverse inclusion, we begin with the fact that $B divides R(G B' G)$
  by
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand.
  Moreover, the indecomposable components of $R(G B' G)_(H times H)$ all have
  the form $(x,y)B'$ for some $(x,y) in G times G$, by
  @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction.
  Since $B$ is a trivial source module (see Exercise
  @exc:block-ideals-trivial-source) we can apply Exercise
  @exc:trivial-source-module-vertices to deduce that $B_(H times H)$ has a
  component $U$ such that $(H times H) inter Delta D <=_(H times H) "vtx" U$,
  since $Delta D$ is a vertex of $B$. By the K-S-A Theorem, we have
  $U tilde.equiv (x,y)B'$, so a vertex of $U$ is given by
  $(x,y)Delta D'(x,y)^(-1)$ for some $(x,y) in G times G$, since $Delta D'$ is a
  vertex of $B'$. It follows that $H inter D <=_G D'$, completing the proof of
  the first statement of part @case:covering-block-defect-intersection.
  #source(504)

  For the second statement,
  $ B divides "ind"_T^(G times G) tilde(B)' $
  by @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal@case:normal-block-induced-bimodule, so a
  vertex $Delta D$ of $B$ satisfies the condition that
  $ Delta D <=_(G times G) T. $
  Using the definition of $T$, we obtain
  $ D <=_G H dot "Stab"_G B'="Stab"_G B', $
  completing the proof of part @case:covering-block-defect-intersection.

  @case:covering-block-maximal-defect By @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal#(
    [@case:normal-block-bimodule-restriction]
      + [–]
      + [@lem:normal-block-generated-ideal]
      + [@case:covering-block-generated-ideal-summand]
  ), the block ideals covering $B'$ are precisely the indecomposable components
  of $"ind"_T^(G times G) tilde(B)'$, where $T$ and $tilde(B)'$ are defined as
  in @lem:normal-block-generated-ideal. Then, for each block $B$ covering $B'$,
  we have
  $ "vtx" B <=_(G times G) "vtx" tilde(B)', $
  by @i:th:vertices-induction-restriction, and by @i:th:vertices-going-up there
  is one block $B_0$ covering $B'$ such that
  $"vtx" B_0 =_(G times G) "vtx" tilde(B)'$. Upon converting these statements
  into the language of defect groups using @prop:block-ideal-diagonal-vertex,
  part @case:covering-block-maximal-defect follows.

  @case:covering-block-centralizer-uniqueness We have $H >= D' C_G lr((D'))$. By
  Theorem @th:brauer-correspondence-centralizer-containment, $B'$ occurs with
  multiplicity one in $(R G)_(H times H)$. It follows that
  $(upright(sans(B))')^G$ is defined, and is the unique block of $G$ covering
  $upright(sans(B))'$. This completes the proof of the theorem.
]

We obtain next some additional information about the defect groups of blocks
$upright(sans(B))_0$ of $G$ satisfying part @case:covering-block-maximal-defect
of Theorem @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks. For this purpose, we assume that
$R$ is part of a $p$-modular system $(K,R,k)$ such that $"char" K=0$, $K$ is
sufficiently large relative to $G$, $R$ is complete in the $frak(p)$-adic
topology, and $k=R slash frak(p)$ is a perfect field of characteristic $p$.
Under these conditions, we know that for an arbitrary subgroup $G_0 <= G$, the
block ideals ${B_0}$ of $R G_0$ are absolutely indecomposable
$R(G_0 times G_0)$-lattices, by Exercise
@exc:block-ideals-absolutely-indecomposable.

#theorem[
  Let $H ⊴ G$, let $B'$ be a block ideal of $R H$, and let $B_0$ be a block
  ideal of $R G$ such that $B_0$ covers $B'$, and $B_0$ satisfies part
  @case:covering-block-maximal-defect of Theorem
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks. Let $D_0$ be a defect group of $B_0$.
  Then
  $ abs(D_0 ":" D_0 inter H)=abs("Stab"_G B' ":" H)_p. $
] <th:maximal-covering-block-defect-index>

We first require:

#lemma[
  (Cline @bib:Cline1973). Let $M$ be an $R G$-lattice such that $M_H$ is an
  absolutely indecomposable $R H$-lattice. Let $D$ be a vertex of $M$. Then
  $D H slash H$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G slash H$.
] <lem:cline-restriction-vertex-sylow>

#source(505)
#proof[
  Let $S$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$. Then $M_(S H)$ is indecomposable, and
  $M divides (M_(S H))^G$ by @i:th:gaschutz-relative-projectivity(iv) since the
  index $abs(G ":" S H)$ is prime to $p$. It follows that if
  $D' in "vtx" M_(S H)$, then $D' =_G D$, so it suffices to show that if
  $D' <= S$, then $D' H=S H$. Since $abs(S H ":" D' H)$ is a power of $p$ and
  $M_(D' H)$ is absolutely indecomposable, the induced module $(M_(D' H))^(S H)$
  is indecomposable, by Green’s Theorem (see Corollary
  @i:cor:normal-p-quotient-indecomposable-induction). Moreover, $M_(S H)$ is
  $D' H$-projective, since $D'$ is a vertex of $M_(S H)$ and $D' H >= D'$. Then
  $M_(S H) divides (M_(D' H))^(S H)$ by @i:th:gaschutz-relative-projectivity
  again. It follows that
  $ M_(S H) tilde.equiv (M_(D' H))^(S H), $
  and we obtain $S H=D' H$ by comparing $R$-ranks. This completes the proof of
  the lemma.
]

#proof(head: emph[Proof of Theorem @th:maximal-covering-block-defect-index.])[
  Let $B_0$ satisfy part @case:covering-block-maximal-defect of
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks, and let $D_0$ be a defect group of $B_0$
  such that $D_0 <= "Stab"_G B'$, by @case:covering-block-defect-intersection.
  Let
  $ T=(H times H)Delta("Stab"_G B') $
  and let $tilde(B)'$ be the block ideal $B'$ viewed as an $R T$-lattice, as in
  the proof of @case:covering-block-maximal-defect. Then
  $tilde(B)'_(H times H) tilde.equiv B'$, and is an absolutely indecomposable
  $R(H times H)$-lattice, by the remarks preceding the theorem. We can now apply
  Lemma @lem:cline-restriction-vertex-sylow, with $tilde(B)'$ in place of $M$,
  $T$ in place of $G$, $H times H$ in place of $H$, and $Delta D_0$ the vertex
  of $tilde(B)'$, by the proof of @case:covering-block-maximal-defect. By the
  lemma, we obtain
  $ abs(Delta D_0 ":" Delta D_0 inter (H times H))=abs(T ":" H times H)_p, $
  using the isomorphism $D H slash H tilde.equiv D slash D inter H$. It then
  follows at once that
  $ abs(D_0 ":" D_0 inter H)=abs("Stab"_G B' ":" H)_p, $
  completing the proof.
]

=== An Extension of Brauer’s First Main Theorem
<ss:brauer-first-main-theorem-extension>

In this subsection, $(K,R,k)$ denotes a $p$-modular system with $K$ sufficiently
large relative to $G$, $"char" K=0$, and $R$ complete in the $frak(p)$-adic
topology. Brauer’s First Main Theorem @th:brauer-first-main-theorem establishes
a bijection between the set of blocks of $G$ with a given defect group $D$ and
the set of blocks of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$. The first part of the
theorem below makes the same statement, with $N_G lr((D))$ replaced by an
arbitrary subgroup $G_0 >= N_G lr((D))$. The proof uses Brauer’s Theorem
@th:brauer-correspondence-centralizer-containment, which is based on the
module-theoretic approach to the Brauer Correspondence. The rest of the theorem
relates blocks of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$ to blocks of
$D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$, using the results of
§@ss:blocks-normal-subgroups, and then to blocks of $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$ of
defect zero. The parts of the theorem, taken together, reduce the study of
blocks of $G$ with defect group $D$, at least to some extent, to the study of
blocks of defect $D$ of the subgroups $D C_G lr((D))$, and ultimately,
#source(506)
to blocks of defect zero of the quotient group $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$. Note
that $N_G lr((D))$ acts by conjugacy not only on the blocks of its normal
subgroup $D$, but also, in a well-defined manner, on the block idempotents of
$R(D C_G lr((D)) slash D)$, using the natural map
$ tau:R(D C_G lr((D))) -> R(D C_G lr((D)) slash D) $
(see @i:prop:normal-p-subgroup-radical-kernel), since $ker tau$ is invariant
under conjugation by elements of $N_G lr((D))$.

#theorem(name: [Extended First Main Theorem])[
  Let $G$ be a finite group, and let $D$ be a $p$-subgroup of $G$.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[Let $G_0$ be an arbitrary subgroup of $G$ containing $N_G lr((D))$.
        Then the Brauer correspondence defines a bijection from the set of
        blocks of $G$ with defect group $D$ to the set of blocks of $G_0$ with
        defect group $D$.]
      <case:extended-brauer-first-main-overgroups>],
    [#case[Each block of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$ covers a unique
      conjugacy class of blocks of $D C_G lr((D))$, each of which has defect
      group $D$.] <case:extended-brauer-first-main-covering>],
    [#case[Let $tau:R(D C_G lr((D))) -> R(D C_G lr((D)) slash D)$ be the natural
        map. Then $tau$ defines a bijection, preserving conjugacy with respect
        to $N_G lr((D))$, from the set of blocks of $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect
        group $D$ to the set of blocks of $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$ of defect
        zero.]
      <case:extended-brauer-first-main-quotient>],
  )
] <th:extended-brauer-first-main>
#idx("Extended First Main Theorem", target: [@th:extended-brauer-first-main])

#proof[
  @case:extended-brauer-first-main-overgroups Since $G_0 >= N_G lr((D))$, we
  have $N_G lr((D))=N_(G_0) lr((D))$. The correspondence of blocks given in
  Brauer’s First Main Theorem @th:brauer-first-main-theorem coincides with the
  Brauer Correspondence, by Exercise
  @exc:brauer-first-main-correspondence-compatibility. Thus the Brauer
  correspondence defines bijections from the set of blocks of $N_G lr((D))$ with
  defect group $D$, with the set of blocks of $G_0$ with defect group $D$, and
  the set of blocks of $G$ with defect group $D$. Now let $upright(sans(B))'$ be
  a fixed block of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$. Then
  $upright(sans(B))_0=(upright(sans(B))')^(G_0)$ is a block of $G_0$ with defect
  group $D$. Since $G_0 >= N_G lr((D)) >= D C_G lr((D))$, the Brauer
  Correspondence is defined for blocks of $G_0$ with defect group $D$, by
  Theorem @th:brauer-correspondence-centralizer-containment. In particular,
  $upright(sans(B))_0^G=((upright(sans(B))')^(G_0))^G$ is defined, and it is
  clear that $((upright(sans(B))')^(G_0))^G=(upright(sans(B))')^G$ (see Exercise
  @exc:brauer-correspondence-transitivity). Since $(upright(sans(B))')^G$ also
  has defect group $D$ by Theorem @th:brauer-first-main-theorem, it follows that
  $upright(sans(B))_0=(upright(sans(B))')^(G_0) ->
  upright(sans(B))_0^G=(upright(sans(B))')^G$
  is the required bijection from blocks of $G_0$ with defect group $D$ to those
  of $G$ with defect group $D$.

  @case:extended-brauer-first-main-covering Since $D C_G lr((D)) ⊴ N_G lr((D))$,
  each block $upright(sans(B))'$ of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$ covers a
  unique conjugacy class of blocks of $D C_G lr((D))$, by Theorem
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks@case:covering-block-conjugacy-class. If
  $upright(sans(B))''$ is a block of $D C_G lr((D))$ covered by the block
  $upright(sans(B))'$ of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$, then a defect
  group $D''$ of $upright(sans(B))''$ is conjugate, in $N_G lr((D))$, to
  $D inter D C_G lr((D))=D$, by
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks@case:covering-block-defect-intersection.
  Since $D$ is a normal $p$-subgroup of $D C_G lr((D))$, the defect group of
  every block of $D C_G lr((D))$ contains $D$ by
  @prop:normal-p-subgroup-block-idempotents and hence $D$ is the defect group of
  $upright(sans(B))''$, completing the proof of part
  @case:extended-brauer-first-main-covering.

  @case:extended-brauer-first-main-quotient Let $A=R(D C_G lr((D)))$,
  $tilde(A)=R(D C_G lr((D)) slash D)$, and let $tau:A -> tilde(A)$ be the
  natural map. As we remarked earlier, $x(ker tau)x^(-1) subset.eq ker tau$ for
  all $x in N_G lr((D))$, so the action of $N_G lr((D))$ on $tilde(A)$ is given
  by
  $
    attach(tau(a), tl: x)=tau(attach(a, tl: x))
    quad "for all" a in A, quad x in N_G lr((D)).
  $
  #source(507)
  We have $tau(c(A)) subset.eq c(tilde(A))$, but the map
  $tau:c(A) -> c(tilde(A))$ is not necessarily surjective, so it is not
  immediate that $tau(b)$ is a block idempotent in $c(tilde(A))$, for a block
  idempotent $b$ in $A$. Nevertheless, we shall prove that this is always the
  case, following an argument due to Landrock @bib:Landrock1983. We first note
  that since $ker tau subset.eq "rad" A$, we have
  $A slash "rad" A tilde.equiv tilde(A) slash "rad" tilde(A)$ by
  @i:prop:ring-radical-quotients-and-modules, so $tau$ defines a bijection from
  the simple $A$-modules to the simple $tilde(A)$-modules.

  Now let
  $ tau(b)=tilde(b)_1+dots+tilde(b)_r, $
  <eq:normal-p-centralizer-block-image>
  for a given block idempotent $b in A$, and block idempotents
  ${tilde(b)_i:1 <= i <= r}$ in $tilde(A)$; we shall prove that $r=1$. Consider
  a projective indecomposable $A$-module $U_i$ belonging to the block ideal $B$
  in $A$ defined by $b$, and assume $U_i$ is not simple. Then all the
  homomorphic images of $U_i$ are indecomposable, and it follows that we have a
  short exact sequence
  $ 0 -> M_1 -> U -> M_2 -> 0 $
  <eq:normal-p-centralizer-linkage-extension>
  with $U$ an indecomposable homomorphic image of $U_i$, and $M_1$ and $M_2$
  simple modules, all belonging to the block $B$. If, for each such sequence,
  $tilde(M)_1$ and $tilde(M)_2$ belong to the same block of $tilde(A)$, then all
  simple composition factors of $tau(b)tilde(A)$ belong to a single block, by
  the theory of linkage (see §@ss:pim-block-linkage), and it follows that
  $tau(b)$ is a block idempotent of $tilde(A)$, as required. So let us assume
  that $r>1$ in @eq:normal-p-centralizer-block-image. Then there exists a short
  exact sequence @eq:normal-p-centralizer-linkage-extension with the property
  that $tilde(M)_1$ and $tilde(M)_2$ belong to different blocks of $tilde(A)$,
  and $U$ is indecomposable. Let us consider the endomorphism $phi=phi_a$ of $U$
  defined by
  $ phi(u)=(1-a)u, quad "for" a in Z(D), $
  where $Z(D)$ is the center of $D$. Since $A=R(D C_G lr((D)))$, $a in c(A)$,
  and $phi in "End"_A U$. Moreover, $phi$ is nilpotent mod $frak(p) A$, since
  $a$ is a $p$-element. Since $frak(p) A subset.eq "rad" A$, $phi$ annihilates
  $M_1$. If $ker phi=M_1$, then $M_2 tilde.equiv "im" phi$, and $M_2$ is
  isomorphic to a submodule of $U$, which is impossible since $tilde(M)_1$ and
  $tilde(M)_2$ belong to different blocks of $tilde(A)$. It follows that $phi$
  annihilates $U$, so $Z(D)$ acts trivially on $U$. Since $D$ is a $p$-group, we
  can repeat the argument, and show that $D$ acts trivially on $U$. Then $U$ can
  be viewed as an indecomposable $tilde(A)$-module with composition factors
  belonging to different blocks of $tilde(A)$. This is a contradiction, and we
  have proved that $r=1$ in @eq:normal-p-centralizer-block-image.

  We next prove that the map $b |-> tau(b)$ is surjective, between the set of
  block idempotents of $A$ and the block idempotents of $tilde(A)$. Let
  $tilde(b)$ be an arbitrary block idempotent in $tilde(A)$; then there exists a
  idempotent $e in A$ such that $tau(e)=tilde(b)$, by @i:th:lifting-idempotents.
  Then there exists a block idempotent $b in A$ such that $tau(b e)!=0$, and
  hence $tau(b)tilde(b)!=0$. Then $tau(b)=tilde(b)$, by the first part of the
  proof, and $tau$ is surjective on the block idempotents. It is then clear that
  $tau$ defines a bijection between the block idempotents of $A$ and those of
  $tilde(A)$.

  Now let us view $A$ as an $G_1$-algebra, and $tilde(A)$ as a
  $tilde(G)_1$-algebra, where
  #source(508)
  $G_1=D C_G lr((D))$ and $tilde(G)_1=D C_G lr((D)) slash D$. Then, from
  §@ss:block-g-algebras-trace and the properties of the trace map, we obtain
  $ b in A_(G_1 slash D) <==> tau(b) in tilde(A)_(tilde(G)_1 slash 1) $
  for all block idempotents $b in A$. Using
  @def:primitive-idempotent-defect-group together with what has been proved
  earlier, we conclude that $tau$ defines a bijection from the block idempotents
  of $A$ with defect group $D$ to the block idempotents of $tilde(A)$ with
  defect group $1$. By @cor:block-defect-group-order, this is the conclusion of
  part @case:extended-brauer-first-main-quotient, and completes the proof of the
  theorem.
]

Our first application of the Extended First Main Theorem is:

#corollary[
  Let $upright(sans(B))$ be a block of $G$ with defect group $D$. There exists a
  unique $N_G lr((D))$-conjugacy class of blocks ${upright(sans(B))'}$ of
  $D C_G lr((D))$ with the properties:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$(upright(sans(B))')^G=upright(sans(B))$, and]
      <case:centralizer-block-correspondence>],
    [#case[the defect group of $upright(sans(B))'$ is $D$, for each block
        ${upright(sans(B))'}$ in the conjugacy class.]
      <case:centralizer-block-correspondence-defect>],
  )
] <cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class>

#proof[
  By Theorem @th:extended-brauer-first-main,
  $upright(sans(B))=tilde(upright(sans(B)))^G$, for a unique block
  $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$. Since
  $N_G lr((D)) >= D C_G lr((D))$, a block $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ of
  $N_G lr((D))$ covers a block $upright(sans(B))'$ of $D C_G lr((D))$ if and
  only if $(upright(sans(B))')^(N_G lr((D)))=tilde(upright(sans(B)))$, by
  Alperin’s Theorem @th:brauer-correspondence-block-summand-criterion and
  Definition @def:covering-blocks. The statement of the corollary now follows
  from @th:extended-brauer-first-main@case:extended-brauer-first-main-covering.
]

#theorem[
  Let $D$ be a $p$-subgroup of $G$, and let $upright(sans(B))'$ be a block of
  the subgroup $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$. Then $upright(sans(B))'$
  contains exactly one simple $k(D C_G lr((D)))$-module, and exactly one
  irreducible $K$-character $zeta$ such that $D <= ker zeta$.
] <th:centralizer-block-canonical-character>

#proof[
  The irreducible $K$-characters in $upright(sans(B))'$ having $D$ in their
  kernels correspond to the irreducible $K$-characters of
  $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$ in the block $upright(sans(B))'_0$ of
  $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$ corresponding to $upright(sans(B))'$ under the natural
  map $tau:R(D C_G lr((D))) -> R(D C_G lr((D)) slash D)$, as in
  @th:extended-brauer-first-main@case:extended-brauer-first-main-quotient. Since
  $upright(sans(B))'_0$ has defect zero by
  @th:extended-brauer-first-main@case:extended-brauer-first-main-quotient, there
  is exactly one such irreducible character, by
  @prop:defect-zero-block-singletons. Now let $F$ be a simple
  $k(D C_G lr((D)))$-module belonging to the block $upright(sans(B))'$. Since
  $D$ is a normal $p$-subgroup of $D C_G lr((D))$, $D$ acts trivially on $F$ by
  Clifford’s Theorem, and hence $F$ is a simple
  $k(D C_G lr((D)) slash D)$-module belonging to the block
  $upright(sans(B))'_0$. Then $F$ is uniquely determined, by
  @prop:defect-zero-block-singletons again, completing the proof.
]

#definition[
  The unique irreducible $K$-character $zeta$ in a block $upright(sans(B))'$ of
  $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$, such that $D <= ker zeta$, is called
  the _canonical character_ of $upright(sans(B))'$.
] <def:block-canonical-character>
#idx("Canonical character", target: [@def:block-canonical-character])

A consequence of the preceding results is that a block $upright(sans(B))$ of $G$
with defect group $D$ is determined by a conjugacy class, with respect to
$N_G lr((D))$, of blocks $upright(sans(B))'$ of $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect
group $D$ such that $(upright(sans(B))')^G=upright(sans(B))$. By
@th:centralizer-block-canonical-character, this conjugacy class corresponds to
the $N_G lr((D))$-conjugacy class of the canonical characters of these blocks.
Their descended characters of $D C_G lr((D)) slash D$ belong to blocks of defect
zero.
#source(509)

We shall use these ideas to define an important numerical invariant of a
$p$-block $upright(sans(B))$ of $G$, with defect group $D$. By
@cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class, there exists a unique conjugacy class of
blocks ${upright(sans(B))'}$ of $D C_G lr((D))$, all with defect group $D$, such
that $(upright(sans(B))')^G=upright(sans(B))$ for each block $upright(sans(B))'$
in the conjugacy class. Let $b'$ be a block idempotent in $R(D C_G lr((D)))$
such that $b'$ defines a block $upright(sans(B))'$ with these properties. Then
$overline(b)'$ is the corresponding block idempotent in $k(D C_G lr((D)))$, and
we have:

#proposition[
  Let $b'$ be a block idempotent in $R(D C_G lr((D)))$, and let $overline(b)'$
  be the corresponding block idempotent in $k(D C_G lr((D)))$. Then
  $ "Stab"_(N_G lr((D))) b'="Stab"_(N_G lr((D))) overline(b)'. $
] <prop:block-idempotent-reduction-stabilizer>

The proof is immediate, by @prop:commutative-order-primitive-idempotents, since
$N_G lr((D))$ permutes the block idempotents of $R(D C_G lr((D)))$ and
$k(D C_G lr((D)))$.

#definition[
  Let $D$ be a $p$-subgroup of $G$, and let $upright(sans(B))$ be a block of $G$
  with defect group $D$. The _inertial index_ $e$ of $upright(sans(B))$ is
  defined by the formula
  $ e=abs("Stab"_(N_G lr((D))) b' ":" D C_G lr((D))), $
  where $b'$ is a block idempotent in $R(D C_G lr((D)))$ associated with any
  block $upright(sans(B))'$ with defect group $D$ such that
  $(upright(sans(B))')^G=upright(sans(B))$.
] <def:block-inertial-index>
#idx("Inertial index", target: [@def:block-inertial-index])
#idx("p-Blocks", "inertial index of", target: [@def:block-inertial-index])

By @cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class and
@prop:block-idempotent-reduction-stabilizer, the inertial index is an invariant
of the block $upright(sans(B))$, and can be defined in terms of block
idempotents in either $R(D C_G lr((D)))$ or $k(D C_G lr((D)))$ which correspond
to $upright(sans(B))$ under the Brauer Correspondence.

The following basic result is due to Brauer @bib:Brauer1967. The proof is an
immediate consequence of the results on covering blocks of normal subgroups
(§@ss:blocks-normal-subgroups).

#theorem[
  Let $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system with $"char" K=0$, $K$ sufficiently
  large relative to $G$, $R$ complete in the $frak(p)$-adic topology, and
  $k=R slash frak(p)$ a perfect field of characteristic $p$. Let
  $upright(sans(B))$ be an arbitrary $p$-block of $G$ with inertial index $e$.
  Then $e equiv.not 0 (mod p)$.
] <th:block-inertial-index-prime-to-p>

#proof[
  Let $D$ be a defect group of $upright(sans(B))$, and let
  $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ be the unique block of $N_G lr((D))$ with defect
  group $D$ such that $(tilde(upright(sans(B))))^G=upright(sans(B))$ (see
  @th:brauer-first-main-theorem). Then $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ covers a unique
  conjugacy class of blocks ${upright(sans(B))'}$ (with defect group $D$), of
  $D C_G lr((D))$. Since $N_G lr((D)) >= C_G lr((D))$, the block
  $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ is the unique block of $N_G lr((D))$ covering
  $upright(sans(B))'$, and hence $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ plays the role of the
  block $upright(sans(B))_0$ in
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks@case:covering-block-maximal-defect. Since
  $D$ is the defect group of $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ and
  $D inter D C_G lr((D))=D$ is also a defect group of $upright(sans(B))'$ by
  @th:normal-subgroup-covering-blocks@case:covering-block-defect-intersection,
  we obtain
  $
    1=abs(D ":" D inter D C_G lr((D)))
    =abs("Stab"_(N_G lr((D))) B' ":" D C_G lr((D)))_p.
  $
  by Theorem @th:maximal-covering-block-defect-index. This proves that the
  inertial index of $upright(sans(B))$ is prime to $p$, as required.
]

=== Brauer’s Third Main Theorem <ss:brauer-third-main-theorem>
#source(510)

In order to apply Brauer’s Second Main Theorem
@th:brauer-second-main-first-form, it is necessary to know, at least in certain
cases, which $p$-blocks of a subgroup $H <= G$ are Brauer correspondents of a
given $p$-block of $G$. This is a difficult problem in general, but has a simple
answer for the important case of the principal block of $G$. The result is
Brauer’s Third Main Theorem @th:brauer-third-main-theorem, which is proved in
this subsection.

We assume throughout the subsection that $(K,R,k)$ denotes a $p$-modular system
such that $"char" K=0$ and $K$ is sufficiently large relative to $G$. For an
arbitrary subgroup $H <= G$, $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$ denotes the principal
block of $H$. By @cor:principal-block-sylow-defect-groups, the Sylow
$p$-subgroups of $H$ are the defect groups of $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$.

In the discussion to follow, we shall make frequent use of the results of
§§@ss:brauer-first-main-theorem-extension, @ss:brauer-third-main-theorem and the
properties of the Brauer Correspondence from §@sec:brauer-correspondence, such
as the transitivity (Exercise @exc:brauer-correspondence-transitivity).

#theorem(name: [Brauer’s Third Main Theorem])[
  Let $upright(sans(B))$ be a block of a subgroup $H <= G$ with defect group
  $D$, and assume that $H >= D C_G lr((D))$. Then $upright(sans(B))^G$ is
  defined, and we have
  $
    upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G)) <==>
    upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H)).
  $
] <th:brauer-third-main-theorem>
#idx("Brauer's Third Main Theorem", target: [@th:brauer-third-main-theorem])
#idx("p-Blocks", "principal", target: [@th:brauer-third-main-theorem])
#idx("Principal p-block", target: [@th:brauer-third-main-theorem])
#idx(
  "Principal p-block",
  "Brauer correspondent of",
  target: [@th:brauer-third-main-theorem],
)

Our presentation follows Alperin @bib:Alperin1986. We first require a
preliminary result, which contains the crucial ideas, and is a special case of
the main theorem.

#proposition[
  The conclusions of @th:brauer-third-main-theorem hold in case
  $H=D C_G lr((D))$.
] <prop:brauer-third-main-centralizer-case>

#proof[
  We are given a block $upright(sans(B))$ of $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group
  $D$. Then $upright(sans(B))^G$ is defined, by
  @th:brauer-correspondence-brauer-map, and
  $upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ if
  $upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$, by
  @cor:brauer-correspondence-principal-block. We now have to prove that
  $upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ only if
  $upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$.

  We begin with the special case in which $D$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$.
  Then $D$ is a defect group of $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$, and
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ is the Brauer correspondent of a unique block of
  $N_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$ by Brauer’s First Main Theorem
  @th:brauer-first-main-theorem. Since
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ by
  @cor:brauer-correspondence-principal-block, this unique block of $N_G lr((D))$
  is $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$. By Theorem
  @th:extended-brauer-first-main@case:extended-brauer-first-main-covering,
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$ covers a unique conjugacy class of
  blocks of $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$. But it is immediate from
  @cor:covering-block-module-criterion that
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$ covers $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$,
  and $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$ has defect group $D$ by
  @cor:principal-block-sylow-defect-groups. Clearly $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$
  is fixed under conjugation by elements of $N_G lr((D))$, and is therefore the
  unique block of $H$ with defect group $D$ corresponding to
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ by the Brauer correspondence (see
  @cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class). This completes the proof for the case
  of a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$.

  We now use induction on the index of $D$ in a Sylow $p$-subgroup to establish
  the general case. Specifically, we assume that if $D'>D$, and
  $upright(sans(B))'$ is a block of $D' C_G lr((D'))$ with defect group $D'$,
  and $(upright(sans(B))')^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$, then
  $upright(sans(B))'=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D' C_G lr((D'))))$. For our given
  block $upright(sans(B))$ of $D C_G lr((D))$ with defect group $D$, let us
  assume that $upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ and
  $upright(sans(B))!=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D C_G lr((D))))$, and derive a
  contradiction. The Brauer correspondent $upright(sans(B))^(N_G lr((D)))$ is
  defined, and has a defect group $D' >= D$ by
  @prop:normal-p-subgroup-block-idempotents. We may assume that $D'>D$, for if
  we have equality, then
  $(upright(sans(B))^(N_G lr((D))))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$, and it
  follows that $D$ is a defect group of $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ by Brauer’s
  first main theorem. Then $D$ is a
  #source(511)
  Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$, and we have
  $upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D C_G lr((D))))$ by the first part of
  the proof. So we may assume that $D'>D$. By Corollary
  @cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class, there exists a block
  $upright(sans(B))'$ of $D' C_(N_G lr((D))) lr((D'))$ with defect group $D'$
  such that $(upright(sans(B))')^(N_G lr((D)))=upright(sans(B))^(N_G lr((D)))$.
  This requires also the observation that
  $D' C_(N_G lr((D))) lr((D'))=D' C_G lr((D'))$ since $D'>D$, and so
  $C_G lr((D')) <= N_G lr((D))$. We then have
  $
    (upright(sans(B))')^G=((upright(sans(B))')^(N_G lr((D))))^G
    =(upright(sans(B))^(N_G lr((D))))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G)),
  $
  using transitivity of the Brauer correspondence. By the induction hypothesis,
  we obtain $upright(sans(B))'=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D' C_G lr((D'))))$. Then
  $(upright(sans(B))')^(N_G lr((D)))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$ by
  @cor:brauer-correspondence-principal-block, and hence
  $upright(sans(B))^(N_G lr((D)))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$. But
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((N_G lr((D))))$ covers only the principal block of
  $D C_G lr((D))$ by @cor:covering-block-module-criterion, and we have
  $upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D C_G lr((D))))$; contrary to
  assumption. This completes the proof.
]

#proof(head: emph[Proof of Theorem @th:brauer-third-main-theorem.])[
  First assume that $upright(sans(B))=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$. Then
  $upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$ by
  @cor:brauer-correspondence-principal-block, in case we have
  $H <= N_G lr((D))$. In the general case, we assume only that
  $H >= D C_G lr((D))$, and have to use deeper properties of the Brauer
  correspondence, from §@ss:brauer-correspondence-general. Since
  $upright(sans(B))$ has defect group $D$ by assumption, $upright(sans(B))^G$ is
  defined, by @th:brauer-correspondence-centralizer-containment. Let $M$ be the
  ring $R$, with trivial $G$-action; so $M$ affords the trivial
  $R$-representation $1_G$. Then $M$ belongs to $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$,
  and the restriction $M_H$ is indecomposable and clearly belongs to
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$. Moreover, a defect group $D$ of
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$, and hence is a
  vertex of $M_H$, by @prop:trivial-module-sylow-vertices. It follows that
  $upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$, by Theorem
  @th:brauer-correspondence-restriction-vertices, completing the proof of one
  implication.

  Now let $upright(sans(B))$ be a block of $H$ with defect group $D$, and assume
  that $H >= D C_G lr((D))$ and $upright(sans(B))^G=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G))$.
  By Theorem @th:brauer-first-main-theorem, there is a unique block
  $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ of $N_H lr((D))$ with defect group $D$ such that
  $tilde(upright(sans(B)))^H=upright(sans(B))$. Then $tilde(upright(sans(B)))$
  covers a unique conjugacy class of blocks ${upright(sans(B))'}$ of
  $D C_H lr((D))$ such that
  $(upright(sans(B))')^(N_H lr((D)))=tilde(upright(sans(B)))$ and
  $upright(sans(B))'$ has defect group $D$, by
  @cor:centralizer-block-conjugacy-class. We also note that
  $D C_H lr((D))=D C_G lr((D))$ since $H >= C_G lr((D))$. Combining these
  results, we obtain
  $
    (upright(sans(B))')^G=(((upright(sans(B))')^(N_H lr((D))))^H)^G
    =((tilde(upright(sans(B))))^H)^G=upright(sans(B))^G
    =upright(sans(B))_1 lr((G)),
  $
  by transitivity of the Brauer Correspondence. Then
  $upright(sans(B))'=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((D C_G lr((D))))$ by
  @prop:brauer-third-main-centralizer-case, and hence
  $upright(sans(B))=(upright(sans(B))')^H=upright(sans(B))_1 lr((H))$ by the
  first part of the proof. This completes the proof of the Third Main Theorem.
]

An interesting generalization of the Third Main Theorem was obtained by Okuyama
@bib:Okuyama1981. His proof (which includes a new proof of the Third Main
Theorem itself) is entirely independent of the preceding discussion, and is
based instead on calculations with characters, using @def:brauer-correspondence
to define the Brauer Correspondence.
